  "notes": "Bluegrass instrumentation played by the artists themselves inside a pop-country mix, with three-part family harmony as the hook device. Two harmonic facts are documented outright and are authored as the top families: \"'Goodbye Earl' C major, progression C–F/C–C–Gsus4 = I – IV/1 – I – Vsus4\" — encoded as I–IV–I–V with a raised `susSubstitutionProb`, because a sus is a quality the numeral parser cannot carry — and the entry's statement that \"the sus4-over-V and the IV-over-tonic-pedal are the signature harmonic moves.\" The documented vocal range G3–C5 is MIDI 55–72 and is the melody register; nothing about it is inferred. Drums are \"mixed drier and smaller than their peers\" with the build being \"drums, fiddle, and soft guitar and piano\" in verses opening to a banjo line at the chorus, so the kit is quiet, cross-stick and shaker carry the verses, and the arrangement rather than the kit does the dynamic work. The structural rule is quoted from the research: \"lines are not repeated in either the verses or chorus,\" so `endVariation` stays on and the melody is authored with a low two-bar cell repeat. ⚠ Boundary flag: the uptempo bluegrass-adjacent cuts push toward the train beat and that groove belongs to `country-train`. ⚠ The catalogue tempo readings beyond the two documented songs are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the drum velocities, the perc density and the countermelody ratio — \"drier and smaller,\" \"cross-stick and shaker verses\" and \"brushes on the acoustic cuts\" are directions, and 96–116 / 1–4 / 0.55 are this author's reading of them.",
